 This method takes optional arguments ``pk=``, ``column_order=``, ``foreign_keys=``, ``not_null=set()`` and ``defaults=dict()`` - explained below.
 
+To create a `STRICT table <https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html>`__, pass
+``strict=True``. STRICT tables reject values that cannot be losslessly converted to
+the type of their destination column::
+
+    db["cats"].create({
+        "id": int,
+        "name": str,
+        "weight": float,
+    }, pk="id", strict=True)
+
+The resulting table uses ``REAL`` for Python ``float`` columns, since SQLite STRICT
+tables only allow the type names ``INTEGER``, ``REAL``, ``TEXT``, ``BLOB`` and
+``ANY``. The ``strict=True`` option is also accepted by ``.insert()``,
+``.insert_all()``, ``.upsert()``, ``.upsert_all()`` and ``.lookup()`` when those
+methods create a new table. The ``table.strict`` property reports whether an
+existing table is strict.
+
